# git-story.hal — the life of a git repository, told as an animation and a poster.
# Run it inside any repo:   hal -d path/to/git-story.hal
# Or point it at one:       hal -d examples/git-story.hal ../some-repo

The repo is the path in the first argument, or the folder I run this
from. If that is not a git repository, say so in one friendly line and
exit 1. git may refuse a repo owned by another user; mark it as a safe
directory first.

Read the whole history: every commit's time, author, and the files it
touched with lines added and removed. Merge commits count as commits but
not toward lines changed. Fold together authors that are clearly the same
person (same email, or same name with different emails).

Part 1, in the terminal, as an animation about 15 seconds long:
    A title card with the repo name, its age, and its first commit message.
    Then a timeline that grows left to right, one column per week (or per
    day for repos younger than two months). Column height is the number
    of commits. Color it from cool blue (quiet) to bright orange (busy).
    While it grows, show the month and year under the playhead, and pop
    up a short label at notable moments: the first commit, the busiest
    week, the biggest single commit, and long silences (over 30 days).
    Finish with a leaderboard: top 5 authors with commit counts and a
    bar each, then the 5 files changed most often.
    Use 24-bit color. Redraw in place, one write per frame, no flicker.

Part 2, write story.html in the folder I run this from:
    A single self-contained page, no external files, that looks like a
    printed poster. Dark paper, big type. The repo name, a hero chart
    of commits over time, a heatmap calendar of activity by weekday and
    hour, the author leaderboard, the most-changed files, and three
    "fun facts" computed from the data (longest streak of days with
    commits, the most common word in commit messages, the latest-night
    commit). Charts are inline SVG.

At the end print the full path to story.html.
